em":"Confirm (and note in the plan) that stall_abort_args() config propagates into the promisor lazy-fetch child of git archive and fires on an accept-but-silent socket, or specify a fallback bound (e.g. communicate(timeout=)) that covers the promisor stall regardless.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.8,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["snapshot.py:181-182 argv is `git -C root archive --format=tar sha` — a local object-DB read, not a transport command","stall_abort_args() (git_fetch.py:194-207) emits `-c http.lowSpeedLimit=... -c http.lowSpeedTime=...`, which only govern curl HTTP transport, not the archive subcommand itself","Repro A: 'promisor remote repointed at a TCP black hole (accepts, never replies)' — the stall is in the spawned lazy-fetch child, not archive proper"],"finding":"git archive is a LOCAL command whose network exposure is only the incidental promisor lazy-fetch spawned as a child git process. The plan recommends stall_abort_args() (which arms http.lowSpeedLimit/lowSpeedTime on curl) but does not verify that these git -c config pairs propagate from the top-level `git -c ... archive` into the child promisor-fetch (via GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS) and actually fire against a TCP black hole that accepts the connection but sends zero bytes (Repro A). If they do not propagate/fire, splicing stall_abort_args() onto `git archive` does nothing for defect 1.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f5f3dff44719ecfc6","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"'Constraints on the fix' section + Repro A","norm_id":"na6b15291443d7fab","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Splicing stall_abort_args() onto git archive if the -c config does not reach the promisor-fetch child, or if lowSpeedTime does not fire before any bytes flow on an accept-but-silent socket"],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.3889,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","